__d(function(e,t,a,i,s,r,o){s.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"Science",textbook:"Science \u2014 Textbook for Class VI",chapter_number:5,chapter_title:"Separation of Substances",quiz_type:"MCQ only",mcqs:[{id:"mcq01",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which method is used to separate grain from chaff using wind?",options:["A) Sieving","B) Filtration","C) Winnowing","D) Decantation"],correct_answer:"C) Winnowing",explanation:"Winnowing uses wind to separate heavier grain from lighter chaff. The mixture is thrown into the air; wind blows the light chaff sideways while the heavier grain falls straight down. This method is commonly used by farmers in Karnataka to clean ragi and rice after threshing."},{id:"mcq02",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following methods would you use to separate stones from rice?",options:["A) Filtration","B) Hand-picking","C) Evaporation","D) Winnowing"],correct_answer:"B) Hand-picking",explanation:"Stones are large, visible, and distinctly different from rice grains, so they can be easily removed by hand \u2014 this is hand-picking. Filtration is used for liquid-solid mixtures. Evaporation recovers dissolved solids. Winnowing uses wind for grain-chaff separation."},{id:"mcq03",difficulty:"Easy",question:"A sieve is best used to separate:",options:["A) Salt from water","B) Particles of different sizes","C) Grain from chaff","D) Sand that has settled in water"],correct_answer:"B) Particles of different sizes",explanation:"Sieving uses a mesh with holes of a specific size to separate particles. Smaller particles pass through; larger ones remain. It is used for separating fine flour from coarse bran, sand from gravel, etc. For salt from water, evaporation is needed; for grain from chaff, winnowing is used."},{id:"mcq04",difficulty:"Easy",question:"When insoluble particles in water slowly settle to the bottom of a container, this process is called:",options:["A) Filtration","B) Evaporation","C) Sedimentation","D) Decantation"],correct_answer:"C) Sedimentation",explanation:"Sedimentation is the settling of insoluble solid particles to the bottom of a liquid under the force of gravity, when the mixture is left undisturbed. The settled solid is the sediment. Filtration uses filter paper; evaporation removes liquid by heating; decantation is pouring off the clear liquid after sedimentation."},{id:"mcq05",difficulty:"Easy",question:"After sedimentation, carefully pouring off the clear liquid above without disturbing the settled solid is called:",options:["A) Sieving","B) Threshing","C) Filtration","D) Decantation"],correct_answer:"D) Decantation",explanation:"Decantation is the careful pouring off of the clear supernatant liquid above the sediment without disturbing the settled solid. It follows sedimentation. The typical sequence for purifying muddy water is: sedimentation \u2192 decantation \u2192 filtration."},{id:"mcq06",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which separation method is used to obtain salt from seawater?",options:["A) Filtration","B) Sedimentation","C) Evaporation","D) Hand-picking"],correct_answer:"C) Evaporation",explanation:"Salt is dissolved in seawater. To separate it, the water must be removed \u2014 this is done by evaporation. Seawater is collected in shallow salt pans; sunlight heats the water, which evaporates, leaving salt crystals behind. Salt pans along the Karnataka coast at Karwar use this method."},{id:"mcq07",difficulty:"Easy",question:"The liquid that passes through the filter paper during filtration is called the:",options:["A) Sediment","B) Residue","C) Filtrate","D) Solute"],correct_answer:"C) Filtrate",explanation:"The filtrate is the liquid that successfully passes through the filter paper during filtration. The solid that remains on top of the filter paper is called the residue. These are key vocabulary terms \u2014 filtrate (liquid through) and residue (solid on filter)."},{id:"mcq08",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of these substances is SOLUBLE in water?",options:["A) Sand","B) Chalk","C) Salt","D) Sawdust"],correct_answer:"C) Salt",explanation:"Salt (common salt / sodium chloride) dissolves completely in water \u2014 you cannot see it after mixing. Sand, chalk, and sawdust are insoluble in water \u2014 they remain as visible particles and do not dissolve. Soluble substances cannot be separated by filtration; evaporation is needed to recover them."},{id:"mcq09",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Which of the following processes happens FIRST in grain processing after harvest?",options:["A) Winnowing","B) Sieving","C) Threshing","D) Hand-picking"],correct_answer:"C) Threshing",explanation:"After harvesting, grain is still attached to the stalk. Threshing (beating stalks to free the grain) happens first. Then winnowing separates the grain from the chaff. Finally, hand-picking removes any remaining large impurities. Order: Harvest \u2192 Thresh \u2192 Winnow \u2192 Hand-pick."},{id:"mcq10",difficulty:"Easy",question:"Why can filtration NOT separate dissolved salt from water?",options:["A) Because salt is heavier than water","B) Because dissolved salt particles are too small to be trapped by filter paper","C) Because filter paper dissolves in water","D) Because salt and water have the same density"],correct_answer:"B) Because dissolved salt particles are too small to be trapped by filter paper",explanation:"When salt dissolves in water, it breaks into individual ions that are far too small to be stopped by the pores of filter paper. They pass through with the water. Only insoluble particles that are larger than the filter's pores can be trapped. To separate dissolved salt, evaporation is needed."},{id:"mcq11",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A student wants to separate a mixture of sand, small pebbles, and gravel. Which method is most suitable?",options:["A) Hand-picking","B) Winnowing","C) Sieving","D) Filtration"],correct_answer:"C) Sieving",explanation:"Sand, pebbles, and gravel differ in particle size. Sieving with sieves of different mesh sizes can separate them \u2014 fine sand passes through the finest mesh, small pebbles through the medium mesh, and gravel is retained on the coarsest sieve. This is exactly how construction materials are graded."},{id:"mcq12",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"The correct order of methods to purify muddy river water is:",options:["A) Filtration \u2192 Sedimentation \u2192 Decantation","B) Decantation \u2192 Sedimentation \u2192 Filtration","C) Sedimentation \u2192 Decantation \u2192 Filtration","D) Filtration \u2192 Decantation \u2192 Sedimentation"],correct_answer:"C) Sedimentation \u2192 Decantation \u2192 Filtration",explanation:"First, sedimentation allows heavy mud/sand particles to settle to the bottom. Then decantation carefully pours off the clearer water above the sediment. Finally, filtration removes remaining fine particles from the decanted water. Each step improves purity. This sequence is used in municipal water treatment plants, including BWSSB in Bengaluru."},{id:"mcq13",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Ravi has a mixture of salt, sand, and water. He wants to get pure salt. What should he do?",options:["A) Filter the mixture, then evaporate the filtrate","B) Evaporate the mixture directly","C) Decant the mixture, then filter it","D) Winnow the mixture"],correct_answer:"A) Filter the mixture, then evaporate the filtrate",explanation:"Step 1 \u2014 Filtration: Filter the mixture to remove insoluble sand (residue on filter). The filtrate = saltwater. Step 2 \u2014 Evaporation: Heat the saltwater until water evaporates, leaving pure salt. If you just evaporated the original mixture, sand would remain mixed with the salt. Filtering first separates the sand, then evaporation isolates the salt."},{id:"mcq14",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which property difference between grain and chaff makes winnowing possible?",options:["A) Colour difference","B) Difference in mass/weight","C) Difference in taste","D) Difference in size"],correct_answer:"B) Difference in mass/weight",explanation:"Winnowing works because grain is significantly heavier than chaff. Heavier grain is barely affected by wind and falls straight down. Light chaff has such low mass that even gentle wind carries it sideways. The key physical property being exploited is the difference in mass (and consequently, how wind affects each component)."},{id:"mcq15",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which statement about evaporation is CORRECT?",options:["A) Evaporation occurs only when water is boiled","B) Evaporation can separate dissolved salt from water","C) Evaporation can separate insoluble sand from water","D) Evaporation gives you the liquid; the solid goes away"],correct_answer:"B) Evaporation can separate dissolved salt from water",explanation:"Evaporation can recover dissolved solids from solutions by removing the liquid (water). The dissolved salt remains when water evaporates. Option A is wrong \u2014 evaporation occurs at all temperatures (just faster when heated), not only during boiling. Option C is wrong \u2014 insoluble sand is separated by filtration/sedimentation, not evaporation. Option D is wrong \u2014 it's the solid that remains, not the liquid."},{id:"mcq16",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Which separation method does a South Indian coffee filter (philterkapi maker) use?",options:["A) Sedimentation","B) Evaporation","C) Filtration","D) Decantation"],correct_answer:"C) Filtration",explanation:"A traditional South Indian coffee filter has a perforated plate that acts as a filter. Ground coffee is placed in the upper chamber. Hot water is poured in and slowly passes through the tiny holes, carrying the dissolved coffee compounds as filtrate (decoction) into the lower chamber. The coffee grounds (residue) remain in the upper chamber. This is filtration in daily use."},{id:"mcq17",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"Karnataka's Karwar coastal region is known for salt production. The method used to produce this salt from seawater is:",options:["A) Sedimentation","B) Winnowing","C) Solar evaporation","D) Filtration"],correct_answer:"C) Solar evaporation",explanation:"Karwar (and other coastal Karnataka areas) produce sea salt by solar evaporation. Seawater is flooded into large, shallow salt pans. Sunlight provides the heat energy to evaporate the water over several days or weeks. The dissolved salt cannot evaporate and crystallises out, leaving large salt deposits that are then harvested."},{id:"mcq18",difficulty:"Moderate",question:"A student pours a mixture of chalk powder and water through filter paper. What will the student observe?",options:["A) The chalk dissolves and passes through the filter paper","B) Both chalk and water pass through the filter paper","C) Chalk stays on the filter paper; clear water passes through","D) Neither chalk nor water can pass through the filter paper"],correct_answer:"C) Chalk stays on the filter paper; clear water passes through",explanation:"Chalk is insoluble in water \u2014 it does not dissolve and remains as solid particles. These particles are too large to pass through the pores of filter paper and are retained as the residue. Water is liquid and passes through the filter paper as the filtrate. This is a standard example of filtration separating an insoluble solid from a liquid."},{id:"mcq19",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"A mixture contains: dissolved sugar + fine sand + water. Which sequence of methods will give pure sugar?",options:["A) Winnow \u2192 Evaporate","B) Filter \u2192 Evaporate","C) Sediment \u2192 Decant only","D) Evaporate \u2192 Filter"],correct_answer:"B) Filter \u2192 Evaporate",explanation:"Step 1 \u2014 Filtration: Filter the mixture to separate insoluble sand (residue) from the sugar-water solution (filtrate). Step 2 \u2014 Evaporation: Heat the sugar-water filtrate until water evaporates, leaving pure sugar. If you evaporated first, sand would remain mixed with the sugar. Filtering first is essential to remove the insoluble sand, then evaporation isolates the dissolved sugar."},{id:"mcq20",difficulty:"Advanced",question:"Does separation change the chemical properties of the separated substances? Choose the correct explanation.",options:["A) Yes \u2014 the separated substance has new properties because it was removed from the mixture","B) No \u2014 separation is a physical process; each substance retains its original chemical properties","C) Yes \u2014 heating during evaporation creates new chemical compounds","D) No \u2014 but the physical properties like colour and texture change after separation"],correct_answer:"B) No \u2014 separation is a physical process; each substance retains its original chemical properties",explanation:"Separation methods (hand-picking, winnowing, sieving, sedimentation, decantation, filtration, evaporation) are all physical processes. They do not form new substances or break chemical bonds. The salt recovered after evaporating saltwater is still ordinary salt (NaCl); the water vapour is still water (H\u2082O). Chemical properties are unchanged. This is what distinguishes physical separation from a chemical reaction."}]}},2715,[]);